nd stitch them  together using that combination that  concatenation operation that i showed  you  and it is fast we're finding that like  this there's a prototype of this  deployed internally that we have staff  shift for python  we're hoping to get that released in the  coming month or two  as a ga release we'll see  but it's fast like we're finding that  index times our median is five seconds  right so you push the content before you  have a chance to alt tab over to your  browser we have indexed that and stored  the partial paths in the database for  later querying  and the stitching algorithm is fast  enough that we're meeting those 100  millisecond budgets that we talked about  earlier our median times are 50  milliseconds our p99s are right around  uh right around 100. so it's working  um i just want to leave you with a  couple of even harder examples i i sort  of  this was a lot of dense content and we  actually have only scratched the surface  of what stack graphs can do so i want to  show a couple of really really hard  examples here so the one on the left is  sort of like our canonical example of  where sometimes lookup results depend on  data flow so if you look at what's  happening pass-through is a function  that takes in a parameter and then  immediately returns that as a new value  and so the  we want to be able to click on one the  reference at the bottom and have it go  to one the definition inside of the  class but in order for that to work we  have to trace the fact that you passed  in a particular parameter and got that  back as a result and so different  invocations of pass-through will have  different results depending on what  parameters you pass in  and then the example on the right uh is  an even more complex one which is  showing how in java you have generic  classes  and what we want in this example is that  if you were to click on length at the  end you should be able to see that first  off we have some inheritance going on so  we need to go find the very first method  entry set in our superclass  but we also need to see that it's not  just the generic hashmap that we've  inherited from we've inherited from a  particular instantiation of hashmap with  string and string as the key and value  types and so when we go through this  chain of methods we need to propagate  that information correctly so that when  we get to the end we know that it has to  be string.length in particular that  you're calling  and the stack framework works for both  of these examples it makes the  uh  formalism a little bit more complex we  actually have this thing called a scope  stack next to the symbol stack as well  which i didn't talk about at all  but it also makes the gadgets that you  create a little bit more complex but  it's still the same overall process  where it's a declarative language for  constructing the graphs and then this  nicely incremental division of work  between index time and query time  and with that that's everything so i  have credits for some of the pictures  that i included  and also like if you have if you're  interested in learning more about this  definitely come check us out in our open  source repositories so thanks very much  [Music]  you
 

